    "episode_id": "case-03d868b86683-0004",
    "evidence": "The claimed total of 42 hours is not supported by the submitted timesheet, whose entries sum to 40 hours.\n\nArithmetic on the document as submitted (employee 4471, weekly timesheet):\n\n| Day | Hours |\n| --- | --- |\n| Monday | 8 |\n| Tuesday | 8 |\n| Wednesday | 9 |\n| Thursday | 7 |\n| Friday | 8 |\n\nSum: 8 + 8 + 9 + 7 + 8 = 40 hours.\n\nThe creator's claim is \"Employee 4471 worked 42 hours this week.\" The only evidence on this record totals 40 hours, which is 2 hours less than the claimed figure. The timesheet itself states that \"No weekend shifts or overtime entries were recorded for this week,\" so there is no entry on the document that could account for the additional 2 hours.\n\nThe claim therefore does not match the record it rests on: the recorded total is 40, and 40 is not equal to 42. I dispute the claim as stated.\n\nUncertainty I acknowledge, in the interest of being precise about what this shows:\n- The document records only daily hour totals for five weekdays; it gives no start or end times, so I cannot verify the individual entries against clock records.\n- The timesheet does not identify the calendar dates of the week in question.\n- The document cannot exclude the possibility that 2 hours of work occurred but went unrecorded; the timesheet is not an externally audited source. However, that possibility is not evidence, and no such entry appears on the submitted record. What the document affirmatively shows is a recorded total of 40 hours, and the claim asserts 42.\n\nThis is a statement about what the submitted record shows, not about employee 4471's conduct or anyone's intent. I am not asking any juror to vote a particular way; each juror should add the hours independently.\n",
